---
import LegalPage from "../components/LegalPage.astro";

const sections = [
  ["local-first", "Local-first operation"],
  ["external-services", "External services"],
  ["contact", "Questions"],
] as const;
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy — Crab"
  description="How the self-hosted Crab UI handles data."
  heading="Crab Privacy Notes"
  lede="Crab UI is an open-source, self-hosted fork. The fork does not operate a hosted Crab account, relay, analytics, or data-collection service."
  effectiveDate="2026-08-31"
  lastUpdated="2026-08-31"
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="local-first">
    <p class="section-number">01</p>
    <h2>Local-first operation</h2>
    <p>
      The Crab deployment runs the UI, server, ACP harness, agent, workspace, tools, credentials, and
      persistent state on infrastructure controlled by its operator. The browser receives the UI and
      the agent events needed to render a session. Review your operator's deployment and access policy
      before sending sensitive data.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="external-services">
    <p class="section-number">02</p>
    <h2>External services</h2>
    <p>
      Crab can connect to model providers, source-control hosts, and other services selected by its
      operator. Those services process data under their own terms. Legacy T3 Connect compatibility is
      retained from the upstream project but is not a Crab-operated service; when enabled, it remains
      an upstream T3 Tools service governed by T3 Tools' policies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="contact">
    <p class="section-number">03</p>
    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      For questions about this fork, use the{" "}<a
        href="https://github.com/fontanierh/crab-ui/issues">Crab UI issue tracker</a
      >. For a particular deployment, contact its operator.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
